      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Exfoliative dermatitis due to calcium channel blockers is a known but infrequent response."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE REPORT",
          "content": "A 77-year-old woman began treatment with diltiazem for angina pectoris. Three days later, severe exfoliative dermatitis developed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "The present case and the others previously reported emphasize the need for greater awareness of the occurrence of adverse cutaneous reactions including toxic epidermal necrolysis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome and cutaneous vasculitis due to calcium channel blockers including diltiazem."
        }
      ]},
